From 63f390bc74cdc312f88e24d70cf71fa9936a81b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 02:45:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 149/274] Kernel preparation: compile declared kernels before
 any distributed work; a startup rendezvous on liveness

DeepSeek V4.1's TileLang modules declare every call they can make
(tilelang/prepare.declare) into the runner's JIT registry while the model is
built. Before memory profiling, the worker compiles them on this rank alone,
in one thread pool (tilelang.prepare), runs the registry's other warmups, and
freezes TileLang's kernel cache: profiling, warm-up, capture and serving
compile nothing, and a call that was not declared fails by name on every
rank alike.

The startup rendezvous waits on the host for as long as every missing rank
is alive: each rank publishes a heartbeat (counter, phase, kernels prepared)
in the distributed store, a waiting rank reports what the missing ranks are
doing, and fails naming a rank whose heartbeat stops changing for
VLLM_STARTUP_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_S (dead or hung). How long a rank prepares is
not bounded; VLLM_STARTUP_RENDEZVOUS_TIMEOUT_MS is gone.

JitWarmupRegistry.registrations() lists what was registered.

Reported-by: huxiaofengtiger (NVIDIA Developer Forums, DeepSeek 4.1 Flash recipe thread, post 24)

